# CSV_QUOTECHAR, CSV_DELIMITER, CSV_ESCAPECHAR and CSV_LINETERMINATOR are
# commented out on purpose, and this is the interesting part of the file.
#
# Their values are a double quote, a comma, a backslash and an escape
# sequence -- that is, exactly the four characters a .env parser uses for its
# own syntax. Written bare, `CSV_QUOTECHAR="` is an unterminated string and
# `CSV_ESCAPECHAR=\` is a line continuation. Quoting them does not reliably
# help either: a lone backslash inside quotes is ambiguous in python-dotenv's
# grammar, and the parser's response to any of these is to report
#
#   python-dotenv could not parse statement starting at line <n>
#
# and then DISCARD THE REST OF THE FILE. Every setting below the bad line
# silently reverts to its default, so the symptom appears as an unrelated
# failure somewhere else entirely -- a database connecting to the wrong host,
# say -- with nothing pointing back here. That indirection is what makes this
# worth a comment block rather than a one-line fix.
#
# Nothing reads these. They are not declared in `app/core/config.py` and
# `extra="ignore"` discards them, and no module in `app/` or `scripts/`
# references any of them. Verify with:
#
#   grep -rn "CSV_ESCAPECHAR" --include="*.py" app/ ../scripts/
#
# So the right thing is for them not to be here. If a CSV import path is
# implemented later, declare these as settings with sane defaults in code --
# a quote character does not belong in an environment file.
#
# CSV_QUOTECHAR
# CSV_DELIMITER
# CSV_ESCAPECHAR
# CSV_LINETERMINATOR


